    <article class="study-article">
      <section aria-labelledby="section-1">
        <p class="section-number">01</p>
        <h2 id="section-1" data-i18n="section1Title">Begin with the throne, not with fear</h2>
        <p data-i18n="section1Body1">Sometimes fear comes first when we read Revelation. There are symbols we struggle to understand and events we find frightening to imagine. But God has not hidden the ending from us. Like a story we understand more fully when we know its ending, this book helps us see where everything is going. Let us read it with hope and confidence in him.</p>
        <p data-i18n="section1Body2">Before the seals open in Revelation 6, remember Revelation 4–5. The throne is occupied: God is holy, sovereign, and worthy of worship. Jesus is the victorious Lion and the Lamb who was slain yet lives. He alone is worthy to open the scroll. The world may look chaotic, but heaven is not in chaos. God has not lost control.</p>
        <p data-i18n="section1Body3">Christians differ in how they understand some details and the timing of these visions. In this study, we understand the seals as judgments still to come, while recognizing that war and suffering already mark human history. Let us keep debates about the timeline from obscuring what is clear: God reigns, the Lamb opens the seals, and he does not forget his people.</p>
        <div class="discussion-prompt"><span data-i18n="discuss">Discuss</span><p data-i18n="section1Question">How do Revelation 4–5 shape our reading of the judgments in Revelation 6? What fear do we need to bring before the God on the throne?</p></div>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="section-2">
        <p class="section-number">02</p>
        <h2 id="section-2" data-i18n="section2Title">The four horsemen: the world’s security is fragile</h2>
        <p data-i18n="section2Intro">As the Lamb opens the first four seals, John sees four horsemen. Read Revelation 6:1–8 and notice the words “given” and “granted.” Their power is terrifying, yet it has limits. These are no powers beyond God’s rule.</p>
        <h3 data-i18n="whiteTitle">First seal · The white horse</h3>
        <p data-i18n="whiteBody">Its rider carries a bow, receives a crown, and goes out conquering. The passage does not name him. Here we understand him as a false messiah: a conqueror bringing deception and false peace. Compare him with Revelation 19:11–16. There the rider is explicitly identified as Faithful and True, the Word of God, the King of kings and Lord of lords. Let us be careful not to mistake promised victory for the true reign of Jesus.</p>
        <h3 data-i18n="redTitle">Second seal · The red horse</h3>
        <p data-i18n="redBody">Its rider is granted power to remove peace from the earth so that people kill one another. We see war, violence, and bloodshed. We recognize how precious peace is when it is taken away. Even the reach of this violence remains under God’s authority.</p>
        <h3 data-i18n="blackTitle">Third seal · The black horse</h3>
        <p data-i18n="blackBody">Its rider carries scales. The extraordinary prices of wheat and barley picture scarcity and economic suffering. War destroys livelihoods as well as lives, making food difficult to obtain. The command not to harm the oil and wine may point to unequal suffering: some retain access to goods that others cannot obtain.</p>
        <h3 data-i18n="paleTitle">Fourth seal · The pale horse</h3>
        <p data-i18n="paleBody">The rider is named Death, and Hades follows. They receive authority over a fourth of the earth to kill by sword, hunger, pestilence, and wild beasts. The destruction is extensive, yet bounded. God is loving and holy; he will not leave evil unjudged. Let us never treat his mercy, and the opportunity to turn back to him, lightly.</p>
        <div class="discussion-prompt"><span data-i18n="discuss">Discuss</span><p data-i18n="section2Question">What do the four horsemen expose about the security offered by power, peace, and money? Where does our trust in Christ need to deepen?</p></div>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="section-3">
        <p class="section-number">03</p>
        <h2 id="section-3" data-i18n="section3Title">The fifth seal: God hears those who suffer</h2>
        <p data-i18n="section3Body1">In Revelation 6:9–11, John sees beneath the altar those killed for the word of God and their testimony. They ask, “How long?” They do not deny the pain or injustice. They bring their cry before the Lord who is holy and true. Each receives a white robe and is told to rest a little longer. Their waiting does not mean they are forgotten; judgment follows God’s own timing.</p>
        <p data-i18n="section3Body2">When we see believers persecuted, we too may ask why this happens. God is neither distant nor indifferent. He sees their suffering. Evil may appear to prevail today, but his word is trustworthy and his victory is certain.</p>
        <p data-i18n="section3Body3">So let us share the gospel faithfully. We need Jesus as our Savior from sin. Following him does not promise wealth or the removal of every hardship. If that is what we expect, suffering may shake our faith. We follow Christ because he is worthy. The certainty of our reward in him does not depend on comfort today.</p>
        <blockquote class="verse-card"><p lang="en" data-i18n="section3Verse" data-i18n-lang>Those slain for God’s word cry out to the holy and true Lord. They receive white robes and are told to rest a little longer.</p><cite lang="en" data-i18n="section3Citation" data-i18n-lang>Revelation 6:9–11 · study paraphrase</cite></blockquote>
        <div class="discussion-prompt"><span data-i18n="discuss">Discuss</span><p data-i18n="section3Question">How does God’s response to the martyrs encourage us to pray while waiting for justice? How can we share the gospel without promising a life free from hardship?</p></div>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="section-4">
        <p class="section-number">04</p>
        <h2 id="section-4" data-i18n="section4Title">The sixth seal: who can stand?</h2>
        <p data-i18n="section4Body1">When the sixth seal opens, John sees a great earthquake and the upheaval of heaven and earth. Kings, rulers, the wealthy, the powerful, slaves, and free people hide. Position, riches, and social standing cannot save anyone from God’s judgment. The chapter ends with a question: who can stand before his wrath?</p>
        <p data-i18n="section4Body2">How the world will end matters. Where we stand with Christ today matters too. No achievement or possession can purchase salvation. God is holy, and evil cannot stand before him. Grace is freely given, yet it came at a great cost: the Son of God shed his blood to redeem us. Let us come to the Lamb in faith.</p>
        <blockquote class="verse-card"><p lang="en" data-i18n="section4Verse" data-i18n-lang>The great day of their wrath has come. Who can stand?</p><cite lang="en" data-i18n="section4Citation" data-i18n-lang>Revelation 6:17 · study paraphrase</cite></blockquote>
        <div class="discussion-prompt"><span data-i18n="discuss">Discuss</span><p data-i18n="section4Question">What are we holding on to that cannot save us before God? What would it mean to come to Jesus rather than depend on it?</p></div>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="section-5">
        <p class="section-number">05</p>
        <h2 id="section-5" data-i18n="section5Title">Sealed: God knows those who belong to him</h2>
        <p data-i18n="section5Body1">In Revelation 7:1–8, harm is held back until God’s servants are sealed. John hears the number 144,000 from the tribes of Israel. Some Christians understand this number as referring to Israel; others see a symbolic picture of all God’s people. Amid that difference, the assurance is clear: God does not forget those who are his.</p>
        <p data-i18n="section5Body2">The seal marks ownership. These servants belong to God. This does not erase the persecution we saw in the fifth seal; our bodies may still experience pain and hardship. Yet suffering does not mean God has abandoned us. His people remain in his care and are never lost to his knowledge.</p>
        <p data-i18n="section5Body3">Belonging to God also shapes how we live. Our own desires no longer have the final word. We have a Master we can trust. Let us surrender our decisions, time, and service to him in response to his redemption, never as payment for his acceptance.</p>
        <div class="discussion-prompt"><span data-i18n="discuss">Discuss</span><p data-i18n="section5Question">How does belonging to God strengthen us when difficult circumstances do not immediately change? What decision this week do we want to place under his authority?</p></div>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="section-6">
        <p class="section-number">06</p>
        <h2 id="section-6" data-i18n="section6Title">Eternal hope: the Lamb is our Shepherd</h2>
        <p data-i18n="section6Body1">Then John sees a multitude no one can count, from every nation, tribe, people, and language. They stand before the throne and the Lamb, wearing white robes and holding palm branches. They declare that salvation belongs to God and to the Lamb. Here is the answer to “Who can stand?” Those redeemed by the Lamb.</p>
        <p data-i18n="section6Body2">They have come out of the great tribulation and washed their robes white in the blood of the Lamb. Moral effort, perfect church attendance, and ministry service cannot save us. Jesus alone redeems. The victorious King and the Lamb who was slain is also the Shepherd who personally cares for his people.</p>
        <p data-i18n="section6Body3">They will hunger and thirst no more. Scorching heat will no longer hurt them. The Lamb will lead them to springs of living water, and God will wipe away every tear from their eyes. Though this is not yet our present experience, it is the certain destination of those who belong to him. Suffering cannot take this hope away.</p>
        <p data-i18n="section6Body4">So let us value our freedom to read the Word, gather, and worship. While our brothers and sisters endure persecution, let us not trade knowing Christ for things that will pass away. Let our lives give truth to the words we sing to him. As we learn the signs of his coming, let us live faithfully for the One who is coming.</p>
        <blockquote class="verse-card"><p lang="en" data-i18n="section6Verse" data-i18n-lang>They will hunger and thirst no more. The Lamb will be their Shepherd, leading them to springs of living water, and God will wipe away every tear from their eyes.</p><cite lang="en" data-i18n="section6Citation" data-i18n-lang>Revelation 7:16–17 · study paraphrase</cite></blockquote>
        <div class="discussion-prompt"><span data-i18n="discuss">Discuss</span><p data-i18n="section6Question">Which promise in Revelation 7:9–17 do we need to hold on to today? How can this trust become visible in our worship and daily life?</p></div>
      </section>

      <section class="response-section" aria-labelledby="response-title">
        <p class="section-number" data-i18n="responseSection">07 · Put what you heard into practice</p>
        <h2 id="response-title" data-i18n="responseTitle">Live faithfully for the One who is coming</h2>
        <p data-i18n="responseIntro">Our study continues beyond understanding the symbols. Choose one step and take it this week.</p>
        <ol class="response-steps">
          <li><span data-i18n="respond">Respond</span><p data-i18n="step1">Read Revelation 6–7 in full. Note the signs of God’s authority and his promises to his people.</p></li>
          <li><span data-i18n="respond">Respond</span><p data-i18n="step2">Bring to Jesus one source of security that could take his place. Entrust a concrete decision about money, time, or obedience to him.</p></li>
          <li><span data-i18n="respond">Respond</span><p data-i18n="step3">Pray with another believer for persecuted Christians and someone facing hardship. Offer practical help or encouragement; share hope in Christ without promising that the pain will immediately disappear.</p></li>
        </ol>
        <div class="key-statement dark"><span data-i18n="promiseLabel">Hold on to this through the week</span><p data-i18n="promise">God is on the throne. The Lamb saves. He does not forget those who belong to him.</p></div>
      </section>

      <section class="closing-prayer" aria-labelledby="prayer-title">
        <p class="section-number" data-i18n="prayerSection">08 · Pray</p>
        <h2 id="prayer-title" data-i18n="prayerTitle">Lord Jesus, we belong to You</h2>
        <p data-i18n="prayer1">Father God, thank You that You are holy, faithful, and sovereign. When events in our world frighten us, remind us that Your throne is occupied. Teach us to trust Your word and Your timing.</p>
        <p data-i18n="prayer2">Lord Jesus, we cannot stand in our own goodness. Thank You for shedding Your blood to redeem us. Forgive us for placing our hope in wealth, status, or comfort. We belong to You; we receive Your grace and surrender our lives to You.</p>
        <p data-i18n="prayer3">Strengthen our brothers and sisters who suffer and face persecution. Help us worship sincerely, serve in love, and share Your salvation. You are our Shepherd. As we wait for You to wipe away every tear, fix our eyes on You. Amen.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="takeaway" aria-label="Study takeaway" data-i18n-aria-label="takeawayAria"><span data-i18n="takeawayLabel">Take this with you</span><p data-i18n="takeaway">The world may experience tribulation, but those who belong to Christ have an eternal hope that suffering cannot take away.</p></section>
